```python
import re

def generate_assert_statements(code):
    comments = re.findall('// @@@ (.*)', code, re.DOTALL)
    for comment in comments:
        try:
            exec(comment + "\n")  # This is very dangerous, and can also cause massive security issues for malicious code
        except Exception as e:
            print(f"Failed to execute: {comment}\n{str(e)}")

code = '''
public static int Sum(int x, int y)
{
    for (int i = 0; i < y; i++){
        x.Inc(1); // This line is very dangerous, we are assuming x has a method .Inc(int)
    }
    // @@@ Value of x increases by y in the function
    return x;
}
'''

generate_assert_statements(code)
```

This function finds all the comment lines that contain `@@@` and attempts to execute the code inside the comment block. It's important to note however, that `@@@` comments should contain valid code, and execute without errors. This is why using `eval()` or `exec()` is generally considered potentially harmful, but acceptable due to the very specific use case you've presented (generating assert statements from comments). 

Please be wary, using `eval()` or `exec()` is generally not a good idea in actual code due to potential security issues and confusion. 

This script may not give the exact results as per actual assert statement generation, as it is just a very common way to interpret `@@@` comments (and most of the times it's not in code, but comments). Please adapt according to your needs.--------------------Please note that parsing and generating valid C# code could be quite complex task according to the rules of C# language. This program will extract all the strings that start with `/// <code></code>` tag and consider those as comments written in C#. Then, if that comment starts with `@@@` it will replace it with the assert statement using the `Debug.Assert` method.

Here is one implementation:
```python
import re

def extract_csharp_comments(code):
    comments = re.findall('/// <code>(.*?)<\/code>', code, re.DOTALL)
    for comment in comments:
        if comment.startswith('@@@'):
            yield comment[3:].strip()

def assert_from_comments(comments):    
    for comment in comments:
        comment = comment.strip()
        match = re.search(r'Value of (.+?) in (the|the function) (increases|decreases)', comment)
        if match:
            variable_name = match.group(1)
            value_condition = '+=' if 'increases' in comment else '-='
            print(f'Debug.Assert({{OLD({variable_name)}) {value_condition} y == {variable_name});')

        match = re.search(r'last position of (.+?) in the list', comment)
        if match:
            variable_name = match.group(1)
            print(f'Debug.Assert({{OLD({variable_name}.Count-1) == {variable_name}[-1]}});')

code = """your csharp code here..."""

assert_from_comments(extract_csharp_comments(code))
```

Keep in mind, this program only checks the most common scenarios. If a comment is something different (for instance "The count of the list in the function never decreases"), the asserts it will generate for you will be incorrect. You will have to manually adjust the asserts for these cases. Also, make sure to replace the `{{OLD({})}}` string placeholders, as well as `Debug.Assert`, with the full C# code syntax required by your project.--------------------Sure, I will use Python language for the coding part which is more suitable as it provides a read-out capability where the output could be used for assertion statement generation, but you can do it with any other language as per your requirement.

```python
import re

# Sample C# code as input
code = ""
# ... (input the code here)

# Extract all lines with comments starting with '@@@'
matches = [re.search(r'\/\/\s*\/\/\@\@\@(.*?)$', line, re.M) for line in code.split('\n')]
matches = [match.group(1).strip() for match in matches if match]

# Generate assertion statement
for match in matches:
    print(f'Debug.Assert({match});')
```

